# 🔐 Day 28 — Linux Security Audit

Today I worked on a basic Linux Security Audit using Bash.

The goal of this project was to understand how Linux systems can be checked for basic security information and possible misconfigurations.

## 🎯 What I Learned

* Linux file permissions
* Users and groups
* Running processes
* Open network ports
* SUID files
* Logged-in users
* Basic Linux security checks
* Bash scripting

## 🛠️ What I Built

I created a simple Bash script that performs several basic security checks on a Linux system.

The script checks:

* Current user
* Operating system information
* Logged-in users
* Running processes
* Open ports
* SUID files

## 💻 Tools Used

* Linux
* Bash
* Terminal
* Basic Linux security commands

## 🚀 How to Run

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone YOUR-REPOSITORY-LINK
```

Move into the project directory:

```bash
cd Day-30-Linux-Security-Audit
```

Make the script executable:

```bash
chmod +x security_audit.sh
```

Run the script:

```bash
./security_audit.sh
```

## 📚 Commands Practiced

Some of the Linux commands I practiced during this project:

```bash
whoami
uname -a
who
ps aux
ss -tuln
find / -perm -4000
```

## 💡 What I Learned From This Project

This project helped me understand that Linux security is not only about knowing commands. It is also about understanding what the output means and identifying things that could potentially create security risks.

I also learned how Bash can be used to automate repetitive security checks.

